      "blocks": {
        "b1": {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "settings": {
            "subheading": "Why Alpine jobs run differently",
            "text": "<p>Alpine sits up against the mountains at the mouth of American Fork Canyon, and the lots reflect it — larger than most of the valley, and very often on a grade. That changes the job. A driveway is longer, a patio needs a retaining edge, and a flat sport court has to be cut into a yard that isn't flat to begin with.</p><p>On an Alpine job the excavation is usually the bigger half of the work. We'd rather spend the extra day on base and grade than come back to a slab that has settled.</p>"
          }
        },
        "b2": {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "settings": {
            "subheading": "One crew, dirt work included",
            "text": "<p>Clearing, demolition, excavation, forming, steel, pour and finish are all handled by the same crew — you're not scheduling an excavator, a concrete contractor and a haul-off service around each other. If your lot needs a grade change first, our <a href=\"/pages/retaining-walls\">retaining walls</a> go in as part of the same job.</p>"
          }
        },
        "b3": {
          "type": "detail",
          "settings": {
            "title": "Grade first, concrete second",
            "text": "On a sloped lot it's almost never the concrete that fails — it's the base under it. We dig, compact and check grade across the whole footprint before a form goes down."
          }
        },
        "b4": {
          "type": "detail",
          "settings": {
            "title": "Built for canyon-mouth winters",
            "text": "Alpine gets more snow and harder freeze-thaw than the valley floor. Joints get cut where the slab wants to crack anyway, and everything drains away from the house."
          }
        },
        "b5": {
          "type": "detail",
          "settings": {
            "title": "Room to work — or not",
            "text": "Big Alpine lots usually mean a truck can back right up to the pour. Where a side yard or a gate is tight, we pump or wheel it in rather than telling you it can't be done."
          }
        }
      },
