    "faq": {
      "type": "faq",
      "blocks": {
        "q1": {
          "type": "qa",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Do you pour concrete in Alpine?",
            "answer": "<p>Yes. We're based in Pleasant Grove, about ten minutes down the road, and Alpine is a regular part of our run. Estimates there are free and there's no travel charge.</p>"
          }
        },
        "q2": {
          "type": "qa",
          "settings": {
            "question": "What does a concrete driveway cost in Alpine?",
            "answer": "<p>It depends on length, thickness, how much grade we have to work with and whether an old driveway has to come out first — and Alpine driveways tend to be longer than average. Use our <a href=\"/pages/estimate\">instant estimator</a> for a ballpark, then we confirm an exact price on site for free.</p>"
          }
        },
        "q3": {
          "type": "qa",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Can you pour a sport court on a sloped Alpine lot?",
            "answer": "<p>Yes — that's most of them. We cut and fill to get a level pad, compact the fill properly so it doesn't settle under the low side, and retain the edge where it needs it. See <a href=\"/pages/sport-courts\">sport courts</a> for how the pad is built.</p>"
          }
        },
        "q4": {
          "type": "qa",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Do I need a permit for concrete work in Alpine?",
            "answer": "<p>It depends on the job. Flatwork inside your own yard usually doesn't; work in the city right-of-way, or anything that changes drainage or grade, often does. We'll tell you what your specific job needs before we start, not after.</p>"
          }
        }
      },
